/**
 * @page topic_network Network and Transport Architecture
 * @ingroup module_network
 * @ingroup network
 *
 * @brief How connections, transports, ACIP, and media admission fit together.
 *
 * @section network_overview Overview
 *
 * ascii-chat separates network work into three layers. Connections own lifecycle,
 * callbacks, deadlines, security state, and packet delivery. Transports own the
 * details of delivery: framing, readiness, backpressure, optional datagrams,
 * and backend resources. ACIP owns packet types, negotiation, authentication,
 * encryption, compression, and dispatch above both. The same protocol handlers
 * therefore work across native and browser-facing transports.
 *
 * The main boundaries are:
 * - `include/ascii-chat/network/connection.h` — connection and server lifecycle
 * - `include/ascii-chat/network/acip/transport.h` — transport contract
 * - `include/ascii-chat/network/acip/protocol.h` — ACIP dispatch and callbacks
 * - `include/ascii-chat/network/packet/packet.h` — packet encoding and decoding
 * - `include/ascii-chat/network/negotiation.h` — connection establishment
 * - `include/ascii-chat/network/acip/send.h` — shared outbound scheduling
 * - `include/ascii-chat/network/http/server.h` — backend-neutral HTTP server contract

 * @section network_http_layer Shared HTTP services
 *
 * HTTP-only services use `http_server_t` and the request/response types in
 * `network/http/server.h`.  The native listener is a bounded libuv service
 * accepting HTTP/1.0, HTTP/1.1, and prior-knowledge HTTP/2 over TCP.  It owns sockets
 * and event-loop callbacks, while handlers own routing and application
 * behavior.  The public contract intentionally does not expose libuv,
 * libwebsockets, TLS, nghttp2, nghttp3, or ngtcp2 types, so MCP, API, and
 * discovery can share the same handler code.  Generic HTTP/3 request streams
 * attach to a pre-opened QUIC endpoint through the same handler contract;
 * WebTransport CONNECT streams remain a separate ACIP transport use of that
 * endpoint.  A supervisor-owned listening socket can be passed to
 * `http_server_adopt_socket()` before start, avoiding a duplicate bind.  An
 * explicitly enabled HTTP version is never silently downgraded.
 *
 * @section network_connection_layer Connections
 *
 * `network_connection_t` moves through `NETWORK_NEGOTIATING`,
 * `NETWORK_READY`, `NETWORK_CLOSING`, and `NETWORK_CLOSED`. Its callbacks
 * run serially outside backend event loops:
 * - `open` allocates application state and may reject startup.
 * - `packet` receives a borrowed payload valid until the callback returns.
 * - `closing` stops and joins application producers.
 * - `closed` releases application state after transport callbacks drain.
 *
 * Use `network_connection_transport()` to reach the transport. Do not keep a
 * socket descriptor, invent a second receive loop, or destroy the transport
 * behind the connection's back.
 *
 * @section network_transport_layer Transports
 *
 * `acip_transport_t` is the stable delivery boundary. Its method table covers
 * send/receive, close, interruption, readiness, capabilities, and backend
 * cleanup. The transport owns its implementation state; application
 * `user_data` is an association owned by the connection or protocol owner.
 *
 * - `acip_transport_send()` and `acip_transport_sendv()` borrow buffers
 *   until the method returns. Queued backends copy data when they retain it.
 * - `acip_transport_sendv_ex()` carries deadlines and datagram preferences.
 *   Unsupported semantics return an error; they are not silently downgraded.
 * - `acip_transport_interrupt()` stops pending I/O without freeing the object.
 * - `acip_transport_destroy()` is an ownership operation, not cancellation.
 *   Stop producers, drain callbacks, and join users before destroying it.
 *
 * @subsection network_backends Backends
 *
 * | Backend | Endpoint or use | Delivery |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | TCP | Native address/port or `tcp://` | Ordered byte stream with ACIP framing |
 * | WebSocket | `ws://` or `wss://` | ACIP packets in WebSocket messages |
 * | WebRTC | Discovery-negotiated peer | DataChannel transport; signaling is separate |
 * | QUIC | `quic://` | Reliable streams and negotiated datagrams |
 * | WebTransport | Advertised `https://` endpoint | HTTP/3 transport backed by QUIC |
 *
 * A backend may expose an underlying socket for event integration, but callers
 * must not bypass the transport contract. WebRTC and WebTransport may not have
 * a useful application socket at all.
 *
 * @section network_packet_flow Packet flow
 *
 * Application code normally sends through a type-specific ACIP helper:
 * @code{.c}
 * acip_transport_t *transport = network_connection_transport(connection);
 * asciichat_error_t result = acip_send_ascii_frame(
 *     transport, frame, frame_length, frame_metadata);
 * @endcode
 *
 * Lower-level code can use `packet_send_via_transport()` when it already owns
 * a packet type and payload. The receive side validates ACIP framing, applies
 * negotiated security and compression, and dispatches the borrowed payload to
 * the registered handler.
 *
 * @section network_admission Media admission and backpressure
 *
 * The shared ACIP outbound scheduler sits between media producers and every
 * transport. It provides bounded admission, ordered control traffic, media
 * dropping under pressure, and encryption sequencing. A mode must not add a
 * second audio queue, video send buffer, or transport send thread.
 *
 * Reliable control packets stay ahead of disposable media. Visual and audio
 * datagrams are used only when the peer negotiated the capability and the caller
 * explicitly requests that delivery mode.
 *
 * See @ref md_docs_2transport-backpressure "Transport backpressure and media admission"
 * and @ref md_docs_2network-copy-reduction "Network copy reduction".
 *
 * @section network_establishment Establishment and adoption
 *
 * `network_connection_open()` resolves an endpoint, chooses its backend, and
 * returns one owned connection. `network_connect_config_t` carries timeout,
 * cancellation, QUIC trust-anchor, and datagram preferences through
 * establishment. `network_connection_run()` drives packet callbacks on the
 * controlling worker.
 *
 * Servers create a `network_server_t` with listeners and callbacks. An already
 * connected or authenticated transport can be adopted with
 * `network_server_adopt()` or `network_server_adopt_negotiating()`; ownership
 * transfers only on success.
 *
 * @section network_security Security
 *
 * Transport selection does not replace application authentication. The shared
 * crypto handshake and identity policy still decide whether a peer is trusted.
 * A WebRTC DataChannel or TLS/WebSocket connection supplies delivery security,
 * but password, key, and ACDS identity requirements remain explicit policy.
 *
 * @section network_shutdown Shutdown
 *
 * Shutdown is staged: stop producers, join them without holding a needed lock,
 * let transport callbacks drain, then close and destroy the owner. A failed join
 * retains live dependencies so a later shutdown attempt can finish safely.
 *
 * Connection, handshake, partial-packet, idle, and cancellation deadlines belong
 * to the connection/backend layers. New network code should not add a blocking
 * `recv()` loop with hard-coded timeout constants in a mode.
 *
 * @section network_low_level Low-level socket helpers
 *
 * Platform socket helpers remain useful inside TCP, WebSocket, and listener
 * implementations. They are not the public protocol boundary. Add new behavior
 * to the connection or transport abstraction first, then use platform helpers
 * only inside the backend that needs them.
 *
 * @section network_discovery Discovery and peer connections
 *
 * ACDS is a signaling and session-discovery service, not a second media
 * protocol. The client uses the shared connection and ACIP runtime to
 * authenticate with ACDS, exchange session metadata, and relay WebRTC SDP/ICE.
 * After peer establishment, media and control packets use the negotiated peer
 * transport; the ACDS connection is not in the media path.
 *
 * @see topic_av
 * @see topic_packet_types
 * @see topic_acds
 * @see md_docs_2networking-runtime.html
 * @see md_docs_2event-driven-io.html
 */
